<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nekhen</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hierakonpolis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nekhen, known to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> as Hierakonpolis, was an ancient city on the west bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a> and one of the largest settlements of the Predynastic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naqada">Naqada</a> period. It was the cult centre of the falcon god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a> and a seat of early kings of Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Its temple deposit yielded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmer-palette">Narmer Palette</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scorpion-macehead">Scorpion Macehead</a>, key objects for the study of Egypt's unification.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nekhen.jpg" alt="Nekhen" width="320" height="528" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ALFGRN · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gebel_el-Arak_Knife_ivory_handle_(back).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
